  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "Solve the modular equation $3x≡5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,14),$ expressing your answer as a nonnegative integer smaller than $14.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use trial and error to find the appropriate multiplicative inverse.*\n\n$x≡$\n{{field-1}}$\\,(\\text{mod}\\,14)$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that a linear congruence $ax≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ has a unique solution modulo $n$ if and only if $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1.$\n\nIn the given congruence, we have $a=3$ and $n=14$, and\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(3,14)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our congruence does indeed have a unique solution.\n\nTo find the solution, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,14).$ To find the inverse of $3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,14)$, we compute the exponents of $3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,14)$ until we get a result of $1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,14):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2} & ≡9 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,14) \\\\ {3}^{3} & ≡9\\cdot 3≡27≡13 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,14) \\\\ {3}^{4} & ≡13\\cdot 3≡39≡11 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,14) \\\\ {3}^{5} & ≡11\\cdot 3≡33≡5 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,14) \\\\ {3}^{6} & ≡5\\cdot 3≡15≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,14)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $5\\cdot 3≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,14),$ which means that $5$ is the multiplicative inverse of $3$ (modulo $14$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our congruence by $5,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & ≡5 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,14) \\\\ 5\\cdot 3x & ≡5\\cdot 5 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,14) \\\\ (5\\cdot 3)x & ≡25 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,14) \\\\ 1\\cdot x & ≡1\\cdot 14+11 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,14) \\\\ x & ≡11 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,14)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x≡11\\,(\\text{mod}\\,14).$",
